\f1\fs32\b The God who remembers and calls\b0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 Torah: Genesis 21:1\u8211?34 | Haftarah: 1 Samuel 1:1\u8211?2:10 | Gospel: Matthew 24:29\u8211?36\par
\f0\fs22\i On Yom Teruah God remembers: He visits Sarah (Gen. 21:1) and remembers Hannah (1 Sam. 1:19) \u8212? the classic reason these very texts are the day's readings. The trumpet blast wakes us to hear: what we received is entrusted to us and carries a calling; giving back is the wakeful act of one who knows from whom it came.\i0\par
\par\f0\fs24 
\par\b\fs28 \u167?0 Observation round\b0\fs24\par


\par\b\fs24 0.1 Blind observation\b0\fs24\par


\par Genesis 21 and 1 Samuel 1 both open with God visiting and remembering the barren woman and giving a son: \u8220?the LORD visited Sarah\u8221? (21:1) and \u8220?the LORD remembered her\u8221? (1 Sam. 1:19). Everywhere there is sound and hearing: Sarah says \u8220?everyone who hears will laugh with me\u8221? (21:6); \u8220?God heard the voice of the lad\u8221? (21:17); Ishmael means \u8220?God hears\u8221?; Hannah prays with moving lips that Eli does not hear, but God hears (1 Sam. 1:13). The day itself is called \u8220?a memorial of blowing\u8221? (Lev. 23:24). And Matthew 24 ends with a great trumpet, the gathering of the elect, and \u8220?of that hour knows no one but the Father\u8221? (24:36).\par


\par\b\fs24 0.2 The four axes as an open question\b0\fs24\par


\par Does the weight fall on what God does (Source: remembering, giving, hearing), on the human response (Will: waking, hearing, giving back), on the wholeness of inner and outer (Coherence), or on the movement through time (Trajectory: promise \u8594? fulfilment \u8594? kingship \u8594? last trumpet)? Do not decide yet; first read the blocks.\par


\par\b\fs24 0.3 The centre of gravity read off\b0\fs24\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8222? Source visibly bears \u8212? God remembers, hears and gives. But the day is called teruah: the sound adds an imperative, to wake up and hear, and Hannah's giving-back and her song make the response active, not passive. Centre of gravity: the God who remembers and calls, and wakes us to hear.\par


\par\b\fs24 0.4 Framing test\b0\fs24\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8908? Guarded. The weekly reflex lands on receiving/entrusting (surrender), and there is real ground: all is gift (21:1; 1 Sam. 1:20). But the day is teruah \u8212? a sound that wakes, an alarm, a coronation blast \u8212? and Hannah's giving-back is a vow-deed, not weary surrender; her song is fierce, a song of reversal. The test therefore does not hold \u8220?receiving\u8221? as the endpoint, but as the ground beneath a wakeful, audible answer. The reflex itself remains under guard.\par



\par\b\fs28 \u167?1 The thread\b0\fs24\par


\par The sound precedes the understanding. God remembers \u8212? He visits, hears and gives \u8212? and that sound wakes us out of \u8220?everything is mine.\u8221? What you received is entrusted to you and carries a calling; giving back is the wakeful act of one who knows from whom it came.\par



