\par\b\fs28 \u167?4 Sources round\b0\fs24\par


\par Fixed order. Van de Giessen now also covers Genesis (1\u8211?25), so the pre-peshat witness is available for the parashah; for the haftarah (1 Samuel) it is still lacking. The other witnesses were verified via Sefaria.\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8222? \b Van de Giessen (pre-peshat, Genesis).\b0  At 21:1 pakad is \u8220?to visit / to attend to\u8221? \u8212? positively: giving Sarah the long-awaited child (cf. Ex. 3:16), while the same word can be negative, \u8220?to visit with punishment\u8221? (1 Sam. 15:2). At 21:6 the wordplay tsachaq (laugh) runs on through \u8220?everyone who hears\u8221? (ha\u353?\u353?omea): laughing and hearing in one verse. At 21:9 he gives for metzachek the sober peshat \u8220?to play, tease, torment\u8221? (Piel participle) \u8212? not idolatry; that keeps Rashi's reading apart as derashah. And he notes the text-critical layer: the passage occurs in 4Q365 (Qumran), with various MT\u8596?SP variants (vv. 7, 8, 10, 13, 14) \u-10179?\u-8223?. At 21:17 the text confirms \u8220?God heard the voice of the lad\u8221? \u8212? the hearing motif stands there itself.\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8222? \b Rosh Hashanah 10b\u8211?11a (Bavli).\b0  By verbal analogy (gezeirah shavah) Sarah and Hannah are \u8220?visited/remembered\u8221? on Rosh Hashanah: pakad (Gen. 21:1) \u8596? pakad (1 Sam. 2:21); zachar (Gen. 30:22, Rachel) \u8596? zachar (1 Sam. 1:19, Hannah); tied to Lev. 23:24, \u8220?a memorial (zikaron) with the blast of the horn.\u8221? This is the classic reason these very two texts are the day's readings \u8212? the remembering is no import but the ground of the day.\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8222? \b Rosh Hashanah 11a (Musaf).\b0  The nine blessings of the Musaf correspond to the nine names of God in Hannah's song (1 Sam. 2:10) \u8212? the tradition binds Hannah's song to the day's note of kingship.\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8223? \b Rashi on Genesis 21:9 (metzachek).\b0  Rashi reads it sharply: idolatry (cf. Ex. 32:6), or immorality, or bloodshed (cf. 2 Sam. 2:14). The peshat (e.g. Ibn Ezra) keeps it soberly at mocking/playing \u-10179?\u-8222?. Point for the reading: Sarah's demand has ground, and teruah too has a sharp, alarming side.\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8222? \b Textual.\b0  Ishmael means \u8220?God hears\u8221? (El shama), and 21:17 says \u8220?God heard the voice of the lad\u8221? \u8212? the hearing motif sits within the parashah. And 1 Sam. 1:13: Hannah \u8220?spoke in her heart, only her lips moved\u8221? \u8212? God hears the voice no one else catches.\par


\par \u9898? \b Onkelos\b0  on 21:1/21:9: still to be verified (open verification point).\par


\par \b Revision gate.\b0  Did the round shift the centre of gravity? Confirming and strengthening: the Talmudic pakad/zachar linkage shows that \u8220?remembering/hearing\u8221? is the classic ground of the day, shifting the accent away from mere \u8220?receiving\u8221? toward \u8220?God who remembers and hears \u8594? waking up to hear.\u8221?\par



\par\b\fs28 \u167?5 Direction test as horizon\b0\fs24\par


\par \u-10179?\u-8907? Confessional. Matthew 24 lets the teruah blast run out toward the last trumpet (24:31): the Son of Man gathers His elect; the God who remembered Sarah and Hannah calls His children together at the end \u8212? no name forgotten. \u-10179?\u-8908? Guarded: \u8220?of that hour knows no one but the Father\u8221? (24:36) keeps the waking sober \u8212? no date-setting, no anxious grasping, but watching with open hands. Peshat guard: Matthew 24 does not determine Genesis/Samuel; it is horizon, not meaning-setter.\par



